That said, here are the most common culprits behind `ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException` in "median of two sorted arrays" implementations, plus a known-good reference you can compare against.

## Common bugs in this problem

1. **Binary searching the larger array instead of the smaller.** The standard O(log(min(m,n))) algorithm requires partitioning the *smaller* array. If you don't swap when `nums1.length > nums2.length`, the partition index `j = (m + n + 1) / 2 - i` can go negative or exceed `nums2.length`.

2. **Wrong partition formula for odd/even combined length.** Use `(m + n + 1) / 2` (note the `+1`) so it works for both parities.

3. **Boundary sentinel handling.** When `i == 0`, `i == m`, `j == 0`, or `j == n`, you must substitute `Integer.MIN_VALUE` / `Integer.MAX_VALUE` rather than indexing into the array. Forgetting any of the four cases throws on edge inputs like `[]` and `[1]`, or `[1,2]` and `[3,4]`.

4. **Binary search bounds.** `lo` should start at `0` and `hi` at `m` (inclusive), not `m - 1`. Off-by-one here breaks when the partition needs to be at the very end.

5. **Integer overflow in `(lo + hi) / 2`.** Use `lo + (hi - lo) / 2` for safety.

## Reference implementation

```java
public double findMedianSortedArrays(int[] nums1, int[] nums2) {
    // Always binary-search the smaller array
    if (nums1.length > nums2.length) {
        return findMedianSortedArrays(nums2, nums1);
    }

    int m = nums1.length, n = nums2.length;
    int total = m + n;
    int half = (total + 1) / 2;

    int lo = 0, hi = m; // inclusive upper bound
    while (lo <= hi) {
        int i = lo + (hi - lo) / 2;     // partition in nums1
        int j = half - i;                // partition in nums2

        int left1  = (i == 0) ? Integer.MIN_VALUE : nums1[i - 1];
        int right1 = (i == m) ? Integer.MAX_VALUE : nums1[i];
        int left2  = (j == 0) ? Integer.MIN_VALUE : nums2[j - 1];
        int right2 = (j == n) ? Integer.MAX_VALUE : nums2[j];

        if (left1 <= right2 && left2 <= right1) {
            if (total % 2 == 1) {
                return Math.max(left1, left2);
            }
            return (Math.max(left1, left2) + Math.min(right1, right2)) / 2.0;
        } else if (left1 > right2) {
            hi = i - 1;
        } else {
            lo = i + 1;
        }
    }
    throw new IllegalArgumentException("Input arrays are not sorted");
}
```

