Here's a fully type-safe generic `EventEmitter` implementation:

```typescript
type EventMap = Record<string, unknown>;
type Listener<T> = (payload: T) => void;

export class EventEmitter<Events extends EventMap> {
  private listeners: {
    [K in keyof Events]?: Set<Listener<Events[K]>>;
  } = {};

  /**
   * Register a listener for the given event.
   * Returns an unsubscribe function for convenience.
   */
  on<K extends keyof Events>(event: K, listener: Listener<Events[K]>): () => void {
    let set = this.listeners[event];
    if (!set) {
      set = new Set();
      this.listeners[event] = set;
    }
    set.add(listener);
    return () => this.off(event, listener);
  }

  /**
   * Register a one-time listener that auto-removes after firing.
   */
  once<K extends keyof Events>(event: K, listener: Listener<Events[K]>): () => void {
    const wrapper: Listener<Events[K]> = (payload) => {
      this.off(event, wrapper);
      listener(payload);
    };
    return this.on(event, wrapper);
  }

  /**
   * Remove a specific listener, or all listeners for an event.
   */
  off<K extends keyof Events>(event: K, listener?: Listener<Events[K]>): void {
    const set = this.listeners[event];
    if (!set) return;
    if (listener) {
      set.delete(listener);
      if (set.size === 0) delete this.listeners[event];
    } else {
      delete this.listeners[event];
    }
  }

  /**
   * Emit an event with its typed payload.
   * Returns true if any listeners were invoked.
   */
  emit<K extends keyof Events>(event: K, payload: Events[K]): boolean {
    const set = this.listeners[event];
    if (!set || set.size === 0) return false;
    // Copy to avoid mutation issues if a listener calls off/on during emit.
    for (const listener of [...set]) {
      listener(payload);
    }
    return true;
  }

  /**
   * Remove all listeners for all events.
   */
  removeAllListeners(): void {
    this.listeners = {};
  }

  /**
   * Number of listeners registered for an event.
   */
  listenerCount<K extends keyof Events>(event: K): number {
    return this.listeners[event]?.size ?? 0;
  }
}
```

### Usage

```typescript
interface AppEvents {
  login: { userId: string; at: Date };
  logout: { userId: string };
  message: string;
  tick: number;
}

const bus = new EventEmitter<AppEvents>();

// Fully typed: payload is { userId: string; at: Date }
const unsub = bus.on("login", ({ userId, at }) => {
  console.log(`${userId} logged in at ${at.toISOString()}`);
});

bus.emit("login", { userId: "u1", at: new Date() }); // ✅
bus.emit("message", "hello");                         // ✅
bus.emit("tick", 42);                                 // ✅

// Compile-time errors:
// bus.emit("login", { userId: 1, at: new Date() }); // ❌ userId must be string
// bus.emit("unknown", 0);                            // ❌ event not in map
// bus.on("tick", (n) => n.toUpperCase());            // ❌ n is number

unsub(); // remove the listener
```

### Design notes

- `Events extends EventMap` constrains the generic to a string-keyed payload map, giving each event its own payload type.
- `keyof Events` narrows event names; `Events[K]` ties the payload type to the specific event being used.
- Listeners are stored in a `Set` per event, so duplicates are deduped and removal is O(1).
- `emit` iterates over a copy of the set, which prevents bugs when a listener calls `on`/`off` during dispatch.
- `on` returns an unsubscribe function, a common ergonomic pattern that avoids needing to keep a reference to the original listener.
- Errors thrown inside listeners propagate to the `emit` caller. If you want isolation, wrap the call in `try/catch` and forward errors to an `error` event, mirroring Node's `EventEmitter`.

## The Problem

The classic mistake is `await`-ing each call on its own line. Each `await` pauses execution until that promise resolves before the next call even starts.

### Sequential (broken) version

```javascript
async function fetchAll() {
  const users = await fetch('/api/users').then(r => r.json());
  const posts = await fetch('/api/posts').then(r => r.json());
  const comments = await fetch('/api/comments').then(r => r.json());

  return { users, posts, comments };
}
```

Total time ≈ `t(users) + t(posts) + t(comments)`. Each request waits for the previous one to finish.

## The Fix

Kick off all three requests first (so they're in flight), then `await` them together with `Promise.all`.

```javascript
async function fetchAll() {
  const [users, posts, comments] = await Promise.all([
    fetch('/api/users').then(r => r.json()),
    fetch('/api/posts').then(r => r.json()),
    fetch('/api/comments').then(r => r.json()),
  ]);

  return { users, posts, comments };
}
```

Total time ≈ `max(t(users), t(posts), t(comments))`.

### Why this works

Calling `fetch(...)` returns a promise immediately and the request starts. By creating all three promises before any `await`, the network calls run concurrently. `Promise.all` then waits for the whole batch.

The same pattern using intermediate variables makes the intent explicit:

```javascript
async function fetchAll() {
  const usersPromise    = fetch('/api/users').then(r => r.json());
  const postsPromise    = fetch('/api/posts').then(r => r.json());
  const commentsPromise = fetch('/api/comments').then(r => r.json());

  const [users, posts, comments] = await Promise.all([
    usersPromise, postsPromise, commentsPromise,
  ]);

  return { users, posts, comments };
}
```

## Production-Ready Version

A few extras worth adding in real code:

```javascript
async function fetchAll(signal) {
  const json = async (url) => {
    const res = await fetch(url, { signal });
    if (!res.ok) throw new Error(`${url} failed: ${res.status}`);
    return res.json();
  };

  // Promise.allSettled if you want partial results instead of fail-fast
  const [users, posts, comments] = await Promise.all([
    json('/api/users'),
    json('/api/posts'),
    json('/api/comments'),
  ]);

  return { users, posts, comments };
}
```

Notes:
- `Promise.all` rejects as soon as any one call fails. Use `Promise.allSettled` if you'd rather get all results back regardless and handle failures per call.
- Pass an `AbortSignal` if callers may want to cancel.
- Check `res.ok`, since `fetch` only rejects on network errors, not HTTP 4xx/5xx.

## Quick Diagnostic Checklist

When you see "supposed to be parallel but isn't," look for:
1. Multiple `await` statements on separate lines, each starting an independent call.
2. `for (... of ...)` loops with `await` inside (use `Promise.all(arr.map(...))` instead).
3. `.then()` chains where the next call only starts inside the previous `.then` callback.
